name: bytes
boss: Void
flavor: Nothing you deleted is as gone as you think.
brief: Text tools stop at the first byte that is not text. hexdump and od show
brief: you what is really there. dd moves raw blocks with no interpretation at
brief: all, which is why it can copy a whole disk -- and why a typo in its
brief: arguments is the most expensive typo on this system.
brief:
brief: dd names its arguments with = rather than flags: if= in, of= out, bs=
brief: block size, count= how many blocks.
---
q: show @bin@ as hex bytes with the printable characters beside them
a: hexdump -C @bin@
out: \|
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -C
hint: C is the canonical format, and the only one worth memorising
---
q: show @bin@ as one-byte octal values
a: hexdump -b @bin@
out: [0-7]
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -b
---
q: show @bin@ as characters, escaping the unprintable ones
a: hexdump -c @bin@
out: .
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -c
---
q: show @bin@ as two-byte decimal values
a: hexdump -d @bin@
out: [0-9]
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -d
---
q: show @bin@ as two-byte hex values
a: hexdump -x @bin@
out: [0-9a-f]
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -x
---
q: show @bin@ as two-byte octal values
a: hexdump -o @bin@
out: [0-7]
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -o
---
q: dump only the first 32 bytes of @bin@
a: hexdump -C -n 32 @bin@
out: \|
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -n
---
q: dump @bin@ starting 8 bytes in, skipping the header
a: hexdump -C -s 8 @bin@
out: \|
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -s
---
q: dump @bin@ without collapsing repeated lines into a single asterisk
a: hexdump -C -v @bin@
out: \|
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -v
---
q: dump @bin@ using your own output format string
a: hexdump -e '16/1 "%02x " "\n"' @bin@
out: [0-9a-f]
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -e
---
q: dump @bin@ with the format string read from a file
a: hexdump -f fmt.txt @bin@
ref: hexdump
teach: hexdump -f
---
q: dump @txt@ in octal with od, the older tool that does the same job
a: od @txt@
out: [0-7]
ref: od
teach: od
---
q: copy @txt@ to copy.txt using dd
a: dd if=@txt@ of=copy.txt
state: exists:copy.txt
ref: dd
teach: dd
---
q: copy the first 512 bytes of @log@ into head.bin
a: dd if=@log@ of=head.bin bs=512 count=1
state: exists:head.bin
ref: dd
teach: dd
---
q: read 16 bytes from @log@ starting at byte 32
a: dd if=@log@ bs=1 skip=32 count=16
out: .
ref: dd
teach: dd
---
q: make a 1 kilobyte file of zero bytes
a: dd if=/dev/zero of=zero.bin bs=1k count=1
state: exists:zero.bin
ref: dd
teach: dd
---
q: make 32 bytes of random data
a: dd if=/dev/urandom of=rand.bin bs=32 count=1
state: exists:rand.bin
ref: dd
teach: dd
---
q: copy @txt@ with dd, showing no progress or summary at all
a: dd if=@txt@ of=copy.txt status=none
state: exists:copy.txt
ref: dd
teach: dd
---
q: split @log@ into pieces of 2 lines each
a: split -l 2 @log@
ref: split
teach: split -l
---
q: split @log@ into pieces of 100 bytes each
a: split -b 100 @log@
ref: split
teach: split -b
---
q: split @log@ using 3-character suffixes instead of 2
a: split -a 3 -l 2 @log@
ref: split
teach: split -a
